Add CUDA GPU mining backend

The miner is a single-file CUDA program (gpu/) sharing a portable crypto
core with a g++ reference build, so the exact same code can be validated
against Node/OpenSSL on any machine:

- gpu/core.h: SHA-512, SHA3-256, GF(2^255-19) arithmetic (8x32 limbs,
  fully reduced), unified Edwards addition and a 64x16 fixed-base comb
  whose constants (d = -121665/121666, base point, tables) are derived at
  runtime, plus base32, pattern matching and splitmix64.
- gpu/miner_cuda.cu: wave-based kernel execution (~0.25s per launch,
  persistent per-thread PRNG state), comb tables generated by an init
  kernel, JSON-lines protocol (ready/progress/match/stop), adaptively
  sized waves. gpu/miner_cpu.cpp: same protocol on one host thread.
- gpu/tools/generate_tables.py: procedurally generated SHA-512/Keccak
  constants (nothing transcribed by hand); hash-selftest mode compares
  both hashes against OpenSSL for 20 input lengths.
- Node side: src/gpu.js locates/builds-talk to the miner, spawns one
  process per device and re-derives every reported match with Node's
  OpenSSL before it is saved (driver/hardware faults cannot write corrupt
  keys; discarded results are counted). src/cpu.js extracts the worker
  thread source; src/search.js now orchestrates any mix of sources.
- CLI: --gpu [devices], --miner <path>, devices command, benchmark --gpu,
  hybrid runs (--gpu -w N), source-aware banner.
- Tests: fixed Python vector on CPU and GPU, 1000 random seeds and hash
  vectors cross-checked against OpenSSL, protocol end-to-end through
  search(), full GPU search with key round-trip. CI builds the reference
  miner so the miner tests run everywhere.

Measured on a GTX 1650 SUPER: ~5.0M candidates/s vs ~162K/s for the
11-worker Node path (~31x), verified byte-for-byte against OpenSSL.
